Programs, verbs, and agents.

A program is a guest executable stored as an object named programs/<id>.
It runs inside a sandboxed RV32 virtual machine, so a buggy or hostile
program cannot reach outside its sandbox and harm the server. Programs are
written in C or Kobold-Pascal and built with the VM SDK. See help kobold.

A program object points at its image in one of two ways:

  image.path   an ELF on the host, under the runtime's image directory
               (data/vm by default), put there by the SDK build.
  image.vfs    an absolute path in the in-world filesystem, produced by
               @compile. When both are set, the VFS image wins.

A program runs in one of three roles, chosen by how it is built:

  standalone     runs once, does its work, and exits (like greet).
  command verb   runs once when invoked against an object, reads that
                 object, and speaks to the room (like look).
  agent          stays resident, listens for events, and acts on them
                 (like agent). An agent runs with its object's owner's
                 authority, so it must have an owner. Set one with @chown.

A command verb is bound to a word a player types with @setverb. For the whole
loop from source to a working verb, see help addverb.

A standalone program can also be run interactively with @run: the lines you
type go to its standard input and its output comes back to you, so it can host
a prompt or a line-driven utility. See help @run.

See also @compile, @chown, @setverb, @run, help addverb, help kobold.
